:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/32/326290611c8ca441f0d9bcc0439678f0c29302b2784ee4fe65e69a3008ec8fe3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392480}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4ef4dea-31e8-5828-8c0a-fbbd1f3e8b97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/df/df22eea4331c47da3047381ca672fd80ebf75118054316fe88c0e2d96dae0d34.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392477} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e05f68f-1fcc-5b0b-b66c-d5b388883e5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392475}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6d5543e2-0abb-561e-9632-e9b1d00fcc94" :question/math-academy-id "q-42117" :question/problem "What is the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall some of the basic properties of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$:\n\n- The domain of the function is $x\\in [-1,1].$\n\n- The range of the function is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\n- Its minimum value occurs at $x=-1.$\n\n- Its maximum value occurs at $x=1.$\n\n- Its zero occurs at $x=0.$\n\n- The function is strictly increasing on its entire domain.\n\nOf the given functions, the only one that has all of these properties is as follows:\n\n![](images/df/df22eea4331c47da3047381ca672fd80ebf75118054316fe88c0e2d96dae0d34.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392501 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115227 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8feeacff-b07d-5f98-a765-4d8571674a7a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Matrix Product Using the Associative Law"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e619a67-9c5c-5198-a6e6-8fe2cae308d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}55 \\\\ -21 \\\\ 50\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392503} {:answer/id #uuid "26af411f-4369-55ad-9e9b-e90514a5bcc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-68 \\\\ -22 \\\\ 50\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392504} {:answer/id #uuid "d5580b66-a310-537c-a0c5-a20457678669" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-68 \\\\ -22 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392505} {:answer/id #uuid "45d96025-8f3a-50af-9de9-cd071e5ec232" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}68 \\\\ -22 \\\\ 40\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392506} {:answer/id #uuid "a3df1fc7-14b6-532e-b12c-6343f041272c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}42 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 50\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392507}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26af411f-4369-55ad-9e9b-e90514a5bcc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-68 \\\\ -22 \\\\ 50\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392504} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed3d0a23-eac0-554f-905e-6466f6601ed5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392502}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4cbd1e16-e623-56fd-8563-e7c6cd4b5ff2" :question/math-academy-id "q-42258" :question/problem "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix, $B$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix, and $C$ is a $3\\times 1$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $3\\times 3$ matrix, which will require finding $9$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $2\\times 1$ matrix, which will require finding $2$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $BC$ first: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $A$ by $BC:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =A(BC) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-68 \\\\ -22 \\\\ 50\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392508 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115227 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8feeacff-b07d-5f98-a765-4d8571674a7a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Matrix Product Using the Associative Law"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0a43c89-29de-5118-a4d4-3f996c643cb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 & -2 \\\\ 15 & -7 \\\\ 9 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392510} {:answer/id #uuid "c445d736-2b33-5a39-a7be-0a95976a8481" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392511} {:answer/id #uuid "cbc16d9e-74fc-598e-a8df-a8298f3cced4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 & -2 \\\\ -15 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392512} {:answer/id #uuid "b96c80d8-2ac1-5468-adbf-38acb2a30f63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392513} {:answer/id #uuid "49f1aa56-0832-5de3-ba9b-9f00b6b7f69f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392514}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49f1aa56-0832-5de3-ba9b-9f00b6b7f69f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392514} :answer-field/id #uuid "c68e793c-51ab-520a-a8e9-a83467b6243d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392509}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "72c2951d-d6a9-5cec-b270-d342a8fcfdc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-42259" :question/problem "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix, $B$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix, and $C$ is a $3\\times 1$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $3\\times 3$ matrix, which will require finding $9$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $2\\times 1$ matrix, which will require finding $2$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $BC$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $A$ by $BC:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =A(BC) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329640 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115228 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a870c4a-daa5-5df2-8968-d8bf9ac0bd75" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Matrix Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19c7b53a-701a-51a2-a16d-4e21a8a4cc1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327157} {:answer/id #uuid "52c48599-aa51-58b9-b7f4-01369eca46de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327652} {:answer/id #uuid "90f50a2a-111f-54d0-a288-d415a537c42d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328199} {:answer/id #uuid "c36d0ce7-0114-5581-9387-0b4151c0500e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328642} {:answer/id #uuid "ec160af8-f144-50c1-b2ac-330cdc7c76a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329001}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c36d0ce7-0114-5581-9387-0b4151c0500e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328642} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f303ed6-16a7-554a-b9b7-791202045199" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329235}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "005a0156-3d99-59d1-ab99-adf9b9b4c7b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-42264" :question/problem "Given that $A,B,$ and $C$ are $2\\times 2$ matrices, which of the following statements are always true?\n\n- $AB=BA$\n- $(AB)C=A(BC)$\n- $A(B-C)=(B-C)A$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. Matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $AB\\ne BA$ in general.\n\n- Statement II is true. Matrix multiplication is associative, so $(AB)C=A(BC)$ in general.\n\n- Statement III is false. Matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $A(B-C)\\ne (B-C)A$ in general.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329647 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100869 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b494140f-206a-5317-8079-ff719a253140" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Matrices Are Conformable for Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56c993a3-b521-5e4e-9b63-95377e12d7bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327688} {:answer/id #uuid "a3940e2a-5e4d-5111-9aa8-655e9e0cdb0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328350} {:answer/id #uuid "a6e55bac-7903-58ed-9ab9-ceb90373ea31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328384} {:answer/id #uuid "c647be2e-8b5a-5ce5-9a50-22a814f61f15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328674} {:answer/id #uuid "d6a063c4-4c0d-5e6a-9d31-a7b0ca0d2115" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328813}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3940e2a-5e4d-5111-9aa8-655e9e0cdb0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328350} :answer-field/id #uuid "3df7bbd7-c529-5b77-87d0-548dcad75995" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329305}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "628bddc5-7db2-5c2d-bca2-c9a2f11c4f87" :question/math-academy-id "q-42381" :question/problem "$$\nO=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 5 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the matrices $O,P,$ and $R?$\n\n- The product $OP$ is well defined\n- The product $OR$ is well defined\n- The product $PR$ is well defined" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $O$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix, $P$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix, and $R$ is a $1\\times 3$ row vector.\n\nThere are $2$ columns in $O$ and $2$ rows in $P.$ Since the number of columns in $O$ equals the number of rows in $P,$ the matrices $O$ and $P$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{O}_{3\\times \\boxed{\\text{2}}}{\\times }_{✓}{P}_{\\boxed{\\text{2}}\\times 3}=\\text{well defined}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, there are $2$ columns in $O$ and $1$ rows in $R.$ Since the number of columns in $O$ does **not** equal the number of rows in $R,$ the matrices $O$ and $R$ are **not** conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{O}_{3\\times 2}{\\times }_{\\ne }{R}_{1\\times 3}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nFinally, there are $3$ columns in $P$ and $1$ rows in $R.$ Since the number of columns in $P$ does **not** equal the number of rows in $R,$ the matrices $P$ and $R$ are **not** conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{P}_{2\\times 3}{\\times }_{\\ne }{R}_{1\\times 3}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nTherefore, statement I is true, while statements II and III are false."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329650 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110574 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2df217d3-d1d2-54cd-8c93-7925225bfdf3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Values in the Range of the Inverse Cosine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08020323-c5a9-5b42-9d0e-dd6a5b878b40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327000} {:answer/id #uuid "49001fd7-a2ca-5328-aca0-c6263276892c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327571} {:answer/id #uuid "6f7b2933-c87c-5869-886d-bd6249488566" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327901} {:answer/id #uuid "7202ed4c-a3a4-5db0-bb3b-08b9f0401f66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327921} {:answer/id #uuid "98bd83f6-9a85-596c-88fa-8629f4a8ff28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328252}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7202ed4c-a3a4-5db0-bb3b-08b9f0401f66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327921} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d81aa2a-bd62-55a7-ab1c-fab1facf05b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329446}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2388e5a3-ccc0-5a99-a06b-72ab673f984d" :question/math-academy-id "q-42433" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the coordinates of the maximum point of the function $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/d2/d2677563c82c37fd1672a806a18524c97ea5ec17cb4f8ef6d8df23b827ba5e81.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the maximum value is $\\pi $ and it is reached at $x=-1.$ Therefore, the coordinates of the maximum point of the function are $(-1,\\pi ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111171 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110587 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b834b64-9679-56c2-b5e9-ee26a54b5e13" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Values in the Domain of the Inverse Tangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3d0ffb3-c274-5957-9de8-6b860acbbd7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108752} {:answer/id #uuid "a859f75b-ba7d-567a-a4b7-320edbe56d5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108753} {:answer/id #uuid "06641cb4-3269-5b14-99e4-274d3d80801d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108754} {:answer/id #uuid "1801a405-2674-52af-b898-ee70bf4a6b0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108755} {:answer/id #uuid "438502c3-9194-55c3-9467-3f1f20b26e57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108756}] :answer-field/id #uuid "3987922b-2de2-5e1f-94e9-cb6722450467" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108751} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e35db56c-6d09-570d-8c71-4db8c2b1880a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402817} {:answer/id #uuid "7110297d-6425-5fbf-bc5a-176c46d4252b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402818} {:answer/id #uuid "119ff29e-ce5b-5841-a13e-2fef13eeb062" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402819} {:answer/id #uuid "2f972d79-6090-50c8-8e79-4d2b5cd343cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402820} {:answer/id #uuid "a3b08fef-d1fc-55e9-8d7c-26d5325ff5f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402821}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f972d79-6090-50c8-8e79-4d2b5cd343cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402820} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ccc38da-312f-53e0-ac01-49b447fcdbf4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402816}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f1bdc4c9-5c09-5a10-a62f-cd8a566a9012" :question/math-academy-id "q-42438" :question/problem "Which of the following values lies in the domain of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x?$\n\nI. $\\sqrt{3}$\nII. $-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$\nIII. $99\\,999$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/d7/d7984b9098d02bfc2b1e9a422aef919533abce54fbb94117e709c7613b0870a3.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The function is defined for all real numbers.\n\nTherefore, all the given values are in the domain of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092709 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092186 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ad1721d-26a4-5459-bc81-297ff0e4d678" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 3x3 System of Equations By Calculating the Inverse of the Coefficient Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f1c5547-1ac6-53d1-b475-3c1f3a7a7421" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327355} {:answer/id #uuid "cc9bc47f-e4a1-5a48-ad95-9fdc8c30c725" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328727} {:answer/id #uuid "e561604b-c400-5b31-b7b4-38788bbd36a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328943} {:answer/id #uuid "ee8db0ff-6f78-5797-8626-bb072f3b6837" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329018} {:answer/id #uuid "ef36cb4d-9cd4-585e-b2e1-dfd00cf7e9a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329023}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f1c5547-1ac6-53d1-b475-3c1f3a7a7421" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327355} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8241441-5d37-5b80-8d02-80f7b14d12b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329508}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "be9d95c2-a710-5571-987b-8cd6ddd0d0ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-42449" :question/problem "Solve the following system of linear equations by finding the inverse of the coefficient matrix:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x-3+z=0 \\\\ 5y-1+3x=2z+1 \\\\ 3z+2x-1=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind  $x+y+z.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the system in the correct order:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x+z=3 \\\\ 3x+5y-2z=2 \\\\ 2x-2y+3z=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we  write the system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2|\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}|-0+|\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(15-4)+(-6-10) \\\\  & =22-16 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}11 & 13 & -16 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -4 \\\\ -5 & -7 & 10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}11 & -13 & -16 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 7 & 10\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}11 & -2 & -5 \\\\ -13 & 4 & 7 \\\\ -16 & 4 & 10\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}11 & -2 & -5 \\\\ -13 & 4 & 7 \\\\ -16 & 4 & 10\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to the given system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}11 & -2 & -5 \\\\ -13 & 4 & 7 \\\\ -16 & 4 & 10\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}24 \\\\ -24 \\\\ -30\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $x+y+z=4-4-5=-5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329675 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096652 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "45b6a076-8795-5510-aabc-62a925826dd0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Scalar Factors in a Linear Combination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08c24834-8cd4-5bea-b65d-b2c5663746e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327004} {:answer/id #uuid "43f90f38-cbf5-5d7a-920b-1121b5764f3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =0$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327528} {:answer/id #uuid "ab7ece4b-a049-56ac-8617-bdfeb30b3311" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =-3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328419} {:answer/id #uuid "b21ee6fb-0023-5a59-b1cc-92b08b1e5bb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =3$ and $\\mu =2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328478} {:answer/id #uuid "baf8f242-62cb-5b3c-a8e9-9e47509ff9d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328564}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08c24834-8cd4-5bea-b65d-b2c5663746e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327004} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce77403f-2bf1-5fb6-9d50-de0552a7684f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329549}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4f6563bc-e431-538e-8c1d-8eaa7d0e394f" :question/math-academy-id "q-42657" :question/problem "![](images/dc/dc708da9adb4f61161ee1face89403bf1a6ce9762905e8a59b4b3ed9031c817a.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/84/84f8951c4921eca20b00132996dc7b2dfafe047d58574e1822da795e1444c861.png)\n\nIn $△OPR$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OP=3|p|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ has the opposite direction to $p$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OP}=-3p.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $PR=2|q|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ has the opposite direction to $q$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PR}=-2q.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR} \\\\  & =-3p-2q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q=-3p-2q.$\n\nSince $p∦q,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =-2$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329677 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110574 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2df217d3-d1d2-54cd-8c93-7925225bfdf3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Values in the Range of the Inverse Cosine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09120532-94e0-544e-82f2-1bf9bff66e23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327007} {:answer/id #uuid "1e42e95f-712b-5a56-a15d-715693430cb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327195} {:answer/id #uuid "4f41c3c2-7d73-57c0-a776-77a9f803c2a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327623} {:answer/id #uuid "5cd3b79c-f5bc-566d-8e25-ec90fec7b98a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327750} {:answer/id #uuid "83cb8e51-60d2-590e-b195-be701bac2c15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328071}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f41c3c2-7d73-57c0-a776-77a9f803c2a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327623} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5dee8f8-0eb9-56f7-9b8b-ea4b26fd25fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329583}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "66851f3f-81cd-56a2-896f-bd1b46d94ca4" :question/math-academy-id "q-42663" :question/problem "Which of the following values is **not** in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/d2/d2677563c82c37fd1672a806a18524c97ea5ec17cb4f8ef6d8df23b827ba5e81.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The range of the function is $[0,\\pi ].$\n\nTherefore, the value $3\\pi $ is not the range of the function."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329678 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096653 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "feae36e7-ae88-5f3a-957f-e65a92e399ad" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Vector That Satisfies a Ratio in Terms of Known Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e3b19d7-9caa-5865-b36b-62dba7ca1b49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OW}=\\frac{1}{3}u+\\frac{2}{3}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327054} {:answer/id #uuid "12bc31bd-410e-5cec-b3e8-db5ec16e6668" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OW}=\\frac{7}{9}v-\\frac{2}{9}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327095} {:answer/id #uuid "1d8032c3-8159-562d-b85e-ce01f3a9419c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OW}=\\frac{7}{9}u+\\frac{2}{9}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327189} {:answer/id #uuid "ccd9827b-fe26-5576-843d-788fca86322f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OW}=\\frac{2}{3}u+\\frac{1}{3}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328732} {:answer/id #uuid "f6116bad-8f2c-5913-918c-8429cac9d1d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OW}=-\\frac{1}{3}v+\\frac{2}{3}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329092}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d8032c3-8159-562d-b85e-ce01f3a9419c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OW}=\\frac{7}{9}u+\\frac{2}{9}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327189} :answer-field/id #uuid "624e21c0-b503-5eed-93a7-5e549f2e0d0a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329358}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cfe5446c-2b26-5e48-974b-de364027f648" :question/math-academy-id "q-42669" :question/problem "![](images/04/040127594f639248736d4492f1853bcb1e6ef18c37db9242ebe466045713628a.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OU}=u,$ $\\overset{\\to }{OV}=v$ and $W$ divides $\\overset{―}{UV}$ in the ratio $UW:WV=2:7.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{OW}.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OW}=\\overset{\\to }{OU}+\\overset{\\to }{UW}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OU}=u,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{UW}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OUV,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{UV} & =\\overset{\\to }{UO}+\\overset{\\to }{OV} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OU}+\\overset{\\to }{OV} \\\\  & =-u+v \\\\  & =v-u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $UW:WV=2:7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{UW}=\\frac{2}{9}\\overset{\\to }{UV}=\\frac{2}{9}(v-u).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OW} & =\\overset{\\to }{OU}+\\overset{\\to }{UW} \\\\  & =u+\\frac{2}{9}(v-u) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{9}u+\\frac{2}{9}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101412 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100844 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ea83062-5744-52ef-9b3e-f9805ed092fb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to an Equation Containing a Transformed Reciprocal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2affdaad-96aa-5484-a442-927e85d5bc81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "110" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327314} {:answer/id #uuid "3521707d-b15a-5eb1-a674-60c3a068dbef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327393} {:answer/id #uuid "8ebe1f6b-8a8a-56c5-a9de-f051b92a33f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "240" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328176} {:answer/id #uuid "b9883cba-3829-5eae-b3f0-46c20405acfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "210" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328552} {:answer/id #uuid "f84442ad-34a5-5352-9847-b2355864c670" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "160" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329110}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f84442ad-34a5-5352-9847-b2355864c670" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "160" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329110} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b837f60-46c5-55db-be33-a2ded8972ca1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329262}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9d1cd618-3e37-5bc5-a865-78591e0eea79" :question/math-academy-id "q-42788" :question/problem "Consider the following trigonometric equation:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}+{20}^{∘})=2.\n$$\n\nThe general solution of this equation is\n\n$$\nx={a}^{∘}+1\\,{440}^{∘}\\cdot n,\\,x=1\\,{120}^{∘}+1\\,{440}^{∘}\\cdot n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. Which of the following could be the value of $a?$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}+{20}^{∘})=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{4}+{20}^{∘}={60}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n,\\,\\frac{x}{4}+{20}^{∘}={300}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n.\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{4}=-{20}^{∘}+{60}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n, & \\,\\frac{x}{4}=-{20}^{∘}+{300}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n \\\\ \\frac{x}{4}={40}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n, & \\,\\frac{x}{4}={280}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n \\\\ x={160}^{∘}+1\\,{440}^{∘}\\cdot n, & \\,x=1\\,{120}^{∘}+1\\,{440}^{∘}\\cdot n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have that $a=160.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101413 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100844 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ea83062-5744-52ef-9b3e-f9805ed092fb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to an Equation Containing a Transformed Reciprocal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12816130-b7ae-58a5-9f0f-13cc67f21c08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327091} {:answer/id #uuid "6184fdd6-eb33-584c-a47e-2dced8a8cf8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327786} {:answer/id #uuid "907e306a-a246-574c-a744-8bdd110c28f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328195} {:answer/id #uuid "9cc6b643-f6b1-53f2-993d-a1106fb48532" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328288} {:answer/id #uuid "f59c64f1-4d1a-5a50-80ef-df0df82a9e03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329084}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "907e306a-a246-574c-a744-8bdd110c28f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328195} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d377a9e-b5c4-59ea-9955-f41f6b988ee8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329420}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bd51b773-7114-5c60-8ae3-bd4c43f5e27a" :question/math-academy-id "q-42789" :question/problem "Consider the following trigonometric  equation:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x=-2\n$$\n\nThe general solution of this equation can be expressed as \n\n$$\nx=a+\\pi n,\\,x=\\frac{11\\pi }{12}+\\pi n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. Which of the following could be the value of $a?$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n2x=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{11\\pi }{6}+2\\pi n.\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x,$ we get:\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{7\\pi }{12}+n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{11\\pi }{12}+n\\pi \\,\\,\n$$\n\nSo, we have that $a=\\frac{7\\pi }{12}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329685 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100844 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ea83062-5744-52ef-9b3e-f9805ed092fb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to an Equation Containing a Transformed Reciprocal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "355f2d5d-2f1c-5183-9fc4-940e1d62b9a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327398} {:answer/id #uuid "5a18aedc-b98f-5616-95a6-211a5ed885f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327725} {:answer/id #uuid "5e141f5d-18c0-5c23-bef3-f0a6b9aa1a97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5\\pi }{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327763} {:answer/id #uuid "7da3a5ce-9c8e-580e-81aa-cc187abf86f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328019} {:answer/id #uuid "c8868cab-79ad-5d64-b43d-dedd294b0996" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328694}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a18aedc-b98f-5616-95a6-211a5ed885f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327725} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fe60e29-6dc6-56ce-98e0-280765400fee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329452}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "14202e95-52cc-54cc-94ac-037a05937a9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-42790" :question/problem "Consider the following trigonometric  equation:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{12})=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\n$$\n\nThe general solution of this equation can be expressed as \n\n$$\nx=a+6\\pi n,\\,x=-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+6\\pi n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. Which of the following could be the value of $a?$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{12})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{12}=-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi ,\\,\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{12}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2\\pi n.\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{12}-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi , & \\,\\frac{x}{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{12}-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{3}=-\\frac{7\\pi }{12}+2n\\pi , & \\,\\frac{x}{3}=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi  \\\\ x=-\\frac{7\\pi }{4}+6n\\pi , & \\,x=-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+6n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have that $a=-\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329692 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176051 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e2a8cf2-64db-5b2f-b0f7-0c727cf492a9" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Properties of the Secant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a3b4306-55a9-5f15-98c8-f5d60b6a40ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327443} {:answer/id #uuid "3cf0eaf4-db08-533b-aed6-515990875a68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327466} {:answer/id #uuid "72c7c4d8-6511-5726-b3e9-0656b209f8f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327928} {:answer/id #uuid "90fc077c-9a61-5c22-a6a7-607c04c3efe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328200} {:answer/id #uuid "95047154-213d-51aa-9ff0-8ef74040020d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328235}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90fc077c-9a61-5c22-a6a7-607c04c3efe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328200} :answer-field/id #uuid "40a9794c-8ea9-5381-b6f9-0d109bd0dcbf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329312}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3923a242-a394-5f37-9f6d-741dafd435c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-42886" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=sec⁡x?$\n\n- The point $(0,2)$ is in the graph\n- $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ is a vertical asymptote\n- $y=-2$ belongs to the range of the function." :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=sec⁡x.$\n\n![](images/da/da729794a0331383debd75be0a4ce4a9f2d4da3286f50bd4f284868996077412.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. The $y$-intercept of $sec⁡x$ is $1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function $y=sec⁡x$ has asymptotes at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,$ thus $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ is a vertical asymptote.\n\n- Statement III is true. The range of the function is $(-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ),$ so $y=-2$ belongs to the range."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329713 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059655 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec62fd08-5729-5dc4-97b9-1a422f70109e" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression by Expanding Parentheses and Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f4dfaf9-6adc-58e2-af62-11084821e994" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327358} {:answer/id #uuid "88ee277f-7d39-51d6-ae8e-804455cc5483" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328112} {:answer/id #uuid "9908e0fd-b391-508e-8dd9-9f1b7572eaab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328256} {:answer/id #uuid "b457a22e-59a5-554f-b4d6-d662134d1479" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328503} {:answer/id #uuid "d0528873-0750-5fae-828d-e2dd5c23f87e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328766}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f4dfaf9-6adc-58e2-af62-11084821e994" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327358} :answer-field/id #uuid "a085cb5f-e013-5736-9ee5-2fef11903a5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329453}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "770be8a8-209f-5a4a-8f5b-267370ed95fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-43288" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta })=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }}+\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, in the last step, we simplified the right-hand side using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta })=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329715 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188222 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "141d61c7-1d82-52fe-b977-19daa910da9b" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression by Factoring Alternative Forms of the Secant-Tangent Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43371a21-e3c4-54a0-9206-f820869cdf7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{tan}^{4}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327520} {:answer/id #uuid "5b837a3e-7895-581c-9547-40fad30c41d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327738} {:answer/id #uuid "7323b252-fad1-5e66-a205-f98c81464280" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{tan}^{4}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327932} {:answer/id #uuid "a0d1151f-bdbb-5b1c-9ec7-0cc63db29b89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328329} {:answer/id #uuid "f467c0f8-8dd0-5354-a064-9d4356dc82bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329073}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "43371a21-e3c4-54a0-9206-f820869cdf7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{tan}^{4}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327520} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5d3df98-9ad8-500d-858a-a65793ee1efc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329533}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "52f9b951-61b8-5687-9876-13fa3e1021d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-43292" :question/problem "${csc}^{2}⁡\\theta {tan}^{4}⁡\\theta -{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following identities: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\n$$\n\nUsing the above identity, we can simplify our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta {tan}^{4}⁡\\theta -{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\frac{{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta }-\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta }-\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\cancel{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }}\\frac{\\cancel{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta }-\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }-\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta {tan}^{2}⁡\\theta -{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ({sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can rewrite our expression using $(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ({sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1) & ={tan}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot {tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={tan}^{4}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta {tan}^{4}⁡\\theta -{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ={tan}^{4}⁡\\theta .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329718 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059643 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1bc8c875-3393-5fd6-90cf-87737e16f2f2" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying a Trigonometric Expression Using a Common Denominator and the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16d51a21-90c5-5b54-a6e4-e0b0b8a2ed02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sec}⁡t+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327134} {:answer/id #uuid "40320fe3-c500-5dcb-a7c9-96f211e3f18e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sec}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327498} {:answer/id #uuid "7a98372a-b734-5075-bb5b-a84d747704c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sec}⁡t-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327984} {:answer/id #uuid "95f2320a-4032-50dc-aeeb-456b3b00cb5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sec}⁡t+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328238} {:answer/id #uuid "ace65715-85a6-5e2d-ba3b-d82ca77a9505" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sec}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328434}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40320fe3-c500-5dcb-a7c9-96f211e3f18e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sec}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327498} :answer-field/id #uuid "34fb1090-54c7-5d88-a697-5bc7387e677b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329286}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "db749ad3-a154-5aac-9219-0c39bfac704c" :question/math-academy-id "q-43298" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}+\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}}+\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe put both terms over a common denominator, and combine the two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{1}+\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify using the Pythagorean identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t})=\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329719 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059656 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e85a2d68-d49f-5bf4-ab62-13ad8c35b0d4" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expression Using an Alternate Form of the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d9790e4-77cb-5ff4-ac43-39c78331e808" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327191} {:answer/id #uuid "9939c042-c8aa-5188-bbb4-1c3c3f7a7f48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328257} {:answer/id #uuid "a407281c-48ae-50b3-a5ba-d986468525b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328354} {:answer/id #uuid "a8f911ba-6e39-5c15-993b-525d98cd4ba0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328397} {:answer/id #uuid "e51dd51b-095f-57d7-a137-944306de7d23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328940}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a407281c-48ae-50b3-a5ba-d986468525b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328354} :answer-field/id #uuid "37d7ae64-5756-5393-953b-b3fac5aef0ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329293}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3eb8e400-2919-528a-91cb-5d1221ea0b97" :question/math-academy-id "q-43308" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )(1+\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta )?$" :question/worked-solution "First, use the identity $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )(1+\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta ) & =(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )(1+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =\\frac{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we expand the parentheses in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the numerator using $1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta $ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329721 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105904 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cafdb077-0e02-5e7a-bf78-c3a8aacfb6f7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Angle or Side of a Triangle Given the Area of a Triangle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55cfe684-dc18-5ab1-b164-6d50fade618a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327676} {:answer/id #uuid "8d23ee9d-454e-5907-a98d-d759e11996ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{25}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328151} {:answer/id #uuid "b7359851-720e-5ab4-876a-6265d25f515d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{40}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328528} {:answer/id #uuid "cf531d87-8a79-5162-b024-e25dc07d7e53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328757} {:answer/id #uuid "e7164a97-e526-5ef2-8ced-7e38b414e248" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{35}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328954}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf531d87-8a79-5162-b024-e25dc07d7e53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328757} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbc9b871-5875-5e5c-8a91-d0aec6bf5882" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329619}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "55d7cd50-6834-55b6-a52f-4f6d251b49dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-43339" :question/problem "![](images/85/85b13f2236c5f00da34ea71fdbfc967882fa6e66c347f6ef40c0604391a5d68e.png)\n\nThe area of ​​the triangle $△ABC$ is $15{\\text{ cm}}^{2}.$ Find $m∠A.$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}⁡A,\n$$\n\nwhere $∠A$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB=5\\,\\text{ cm}, \\\\ b & =AC=12\\,\\text{cm}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $m∠A$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot c\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ 15 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 12\\cdot 5\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ 15 & =30\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ \\frac{15}{30} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡A & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ m∠A & ={30}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329723 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129122 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1eeb42af-74ee-53d7-af9f-be171f77968f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Period of a Transformed Sinusoidal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f720e8d-bb1b-59ca-9ab6-dc161291bf7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327071} {:answer/id #uuid "5fc7ca04-3dfc-5466-a866-a199437da7e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327775} {:answer/id #uuid "7851f496-18f4-5ce9-b27a-0a6147c6176e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327963} {:answer/id #uuid "85974f52-6385-50ae-a81b-e8bc2b19d899" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{15}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328085} {:answer/id #uuid "dfb14292-bdff-5641-bc24-4dffec7b4e6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{75}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328894}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7851f496-18f4-5ce9-b27a-0a6147c6176e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327963} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2a5f161-27b6-5dd4-aa10-c1cb6123c7a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329527}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "72d5418e-aa83-5e26-b56d-9365871c6a6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-43411" :question/problem "What is the period of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x+{90}^{∘})?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is ${360}^{∘}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=8.$ Therefore, the period is $\\frac{{360}^{∘}}{8}={45}^{∘}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111178 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110553 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "03491655-9b81-5fc0-bf20-e4f74a9c72ad" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression Using the Secant-Tangent Identity: Replacing a Squared Tangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3181638b-4d66-589a-b061-04d74bf07e12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327372} {:answer/id #uuid "4fd3b151-608d-541d-b9c4-1224e1328c24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327630} {:answer/id #uuid "584096c0-c59b-5b5c-802a-e72a377ffdcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327708} {:answer/id #uuid "5b36cc0f-2f9f-59d1-bc2e-d088509a383f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327734} {:answer/id #uuid "9b4c20ff-9024-5eb1-89f9-dc0efac40673" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328278}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4fd3b151-608d-541d-b9c4-1224e1328c24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327630} :answer-field/id #uuid "bac4f7c2-82a2-5f28-8ff9-2ad956252386" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329515}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a581c5d1-c937-510f-8cbd-8478ab152d79" :question/math-academy-id "q-43617" :question/problem "$\\frac{1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta }=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given expression using the secant-tangent identity as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta }}{\\cancel{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta }=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392554 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110585 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "46e1513d-0944-5c09-8a7e-e1d9ae81bae5" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing the Inverse Tangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "704dd449-03cc-5cb5-8d71-9633864418fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/90/901cc06ce7661348fa18bb172f0a014e0c0d49e6f0a788cfe5926faa6e3061b0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392556} {:answer/id #uuid "ab422978-41b5-5448-9e5c-293965809dac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b2/b2939d0005167bb5771d7a566af50eff3c0d595ff95666d97d0d52597518f814.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392557} {:answer/id #uuid "537f4564-f5f1-5ad4-9cdb-0876e35602eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d7/d7984b9098d02bfc2b1e9a422aef919533abce54fbb94117e709c7613b0870a3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392558} {:answer/id #uuid "3969e61c-1862-53e5-8d39-b6a5cccbc85b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7a/7aadddedfe33accf816c81268b8873d02a324034e6ab26bdbfa9895989e7e63e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392559} {:answer/id #uuid "99f07ab8-6830-58b2-8709-6db297f0d693" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e7/e7aff749f5804fb774a334918b62ff9f20fd8e384fcebbc372e811ce51729d4a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392560}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "537f4564-f5f1-5ad4-9cdb-0876e35602eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d7/d7984b9098d02bfc2b1e9a422aef919533abce54fbb94117e709c7613b0870a3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392558} :answer-field/id #uuid "51b945ad-4e83-57d3-875d-408d14586c43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392555}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e78d2563-d22e-5b86-b89f-f6aab65d4ec5" :question/math-academy-id "q-43659" :question/problem "What is the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall some of the basic properties of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x:$\n\n- The domain is all real numbers.\n\n- There is a zero at $x=0.$\n\n- The function is negative over $(-\\infty ,0)$ and positive over $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n- The function is strictly increasing in its entire domain.\n\n- $y\\to -\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (from above) as $x\\to -\\infty .$\n\n- $y\\to \\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (from below) as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The lines $y=\\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are horizontal asymptotes of the function.\n\nOf the given functions, the only one that has all of these properties is as follows:\n\n![](images/d7/d7984b9098d02bfc2b1e9a422aef919533abce54fbb94117e709c7613b0870a3.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329753 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175922 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae4289af-e251-514c-981e-5c6d21992ad3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Perpendicular Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e922a42-6deb-5bc6-a4f2-a8d06f3fea60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328024} {:answer/id #uuid "d59f7611-aece-5245-9299-86b545be9496" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328803} {:answer/id #uuid "dd0e28cc-1799-536e-bd55-0a06dc16bb67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328867} {:answer/id #uuid "de5640a4-5333-5d9b-b991-e89e51fe95ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-6x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328881} {:answer/id #uuid "ed73643a-713f-5f7a-aea6-c63401f50949" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2x+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329014}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d59f7611-aece-5245-9299-86b545be9496" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328803} :answer-field/id #uuid "2512d590-4b14-5e67-bd6c-bb6c4cebbb08" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329251}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "36e2ce70-a218-5096-a57b-5f93338562ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-44337" :question/problem "![](images/c9/c9c426e75153bd893e3dbef39bd8a837991d8c3ec6a48b41aeb27df6c4250660.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, the equation of the line ${l}_{1}$ is $y=2x-1$ and ${l}_{2}$ is perpendicular to ${l}_{1}.$ What is the equation of the line ${l}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of ${l}_{2}$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of ${l}_{1}.$ Since ${l}_{1}$ has a slope of $2,$ the line ${l}_{2}$ must have a slope of $-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and a $y$-intercept of $6.$ Therefore, it can be written in slope-intercept form as\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{2}x+6.\n$$"}]}
